⭐ 4th Question: Reconstruct Itinerary
🧩 Problem Statement

You are given a list of airline tickets where:

tickets[i] = [from, to]


You must reconstruct the itinerary in order such that:

The itinerary must begin with "JFK"

Use all tickets exactly once

If multiple valid itineraries exist → return the lexicographically smallest one.

🔎 Example

Input:

[["JFK","SFO"],
 ["JFK","ATL"],
 ["SFO","ATL"],
 ["ATL","JFK"],
 ["ATL","SFO"]]


Output:

["JFK","ATL","JFK","SFO","ATL","SFO"]

🧠 Key Concept

This is actually a:

🔥 Eulerian Path in a Directed Graph

We must:

Visit all edges exactly once

Start from JFK

Use:

Hierholzer’s Algorithm (DFS based)

💡 Why PriorityQueue?

Because if multiple destinations exist:
We must choose lexicographically smallest.

⏱ Time Complexity

O(E log E)
(Heap operations)

Space: O(V + E)

💻 Java Code
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    Map<String, PriorityQueue<String>> graph = new HashMap<>();
    LinkedList<String> result = new LinkedList<>();

    public List<String> findItinerary(List<List<String>> tickets) {

        // Step 1: Build Graph
        for (List<String> ticket : tickets) {
            graph.computeIfAbsent(ticket.get(0), k -> new PriorityQueue<>())
                 .offer(ticket.get(1));
        }

        // Step 2: DFS
        dfs("JFK");

        return result;
    }

    private void dfs(String airport) {

        PriorityQueue<String> destinations = graph.get(airport);

        while (destinations != null && !destinations.isEmpty()) {
            String next = destinations.poll();
            dfs(next);
        }

        result.addFirst(airport);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Solution sol = new Solution();

        List<List<String>> tickets = new ArrayList<>();
        tickets.add(Arrays.asList("JFK","SFO"));
        tickets.add(Arrays.asList("JFK","ATL"));
        tickets.add(Arrays.asList("SFO","ATL"));
        tickets.add(Arrays.asList("ATL","JFK"));
        tickets.add(Arrays.asList("ATL","SFO"));

        System.out.println(sol.findItinerary(tickets));
    }
}

🔍 Line-by-Line Explanation
Step 1: Build Graph
graph.computeIfAbsent(...)


Creates adjacency list:

JFK → [ATL, SFO]
ATL → [JFK, SFO]
SFO → [ATL]


PriorityQueue ensures:

Always smallest destination first.

Step 2: DFS (Hierholzer’s Algorithm)
while destination exists:
    dfs(next)


We remove edges as we travel.

Why addFirst ?

Because Eulerian path is constructed in reverse order.

So we insert at front.

🔎 Step Execution

Start:

JFK → ATL → JFK → SFO → ATL → SFO

Edges exhausted → build result backward.

